const body = `<h1>10.5b — Maze Solver: Recursive Backtracking in a Grid</h1>

<div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Season 2: beyond the exam.</strong> This topic is no longer tested
    (the 2025 CED removed inheritance from the exam). We build it after May 12
    because it makes the RPG engine dramatically better, and because you are
    ready for it. Pure craft, zero test anxiety.
</div>



<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Basic Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Requirement 1: Maze Representation</h3>

    <p>A maze is a 2D char array:</p>
    <pre><code><span class="output">S = Start  E = End  # = Wall  . = Open

S . # . .
. # . # .
. . . # .
# . # . .
. # . . E</span></code></pre>

    <h3>Requirement 2: Recursive Maze Solver</h3>

    <p><code>solve(r, c)</code> returns true if a path exists from (r,c) to E:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>If out of bounds → return false</li>
        <li>If wall → return false</li>
        <li>If already visited → return false</li>
        <li>If this is E → return true (found it!)</li>
        <li>Mark as visited (change '.' to 'x')</li>
        <li>Try all 4 directions: up, down, left, right</li>
        <li>If any direction returns true → return true</li>
        <li>None worked → unmark (change 'x' back to '.'), return false</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <p>Step 8 (unmark) is the "backtrack" part. Why is it necessary? If you
        DON'T unmark, and the solver tries a dead-end path first, what happens
        when it needs to try a different route through the same cell? Draw a small
        maze with a T-junction and trace both versions (with and without unmark).</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Requirement 3: Find ALL Paths</h3>

    <p>Modify the solver to find every path from S to E (not just one). Print each
    complete path as a list of coordinates. Count them. For the maze above, how
    many unique paths exist?</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Path vs. Cell Visitation</h3>
        <p>In the ALL-paths version, you can't just mark cells as "visited" and
        never revisit them — different paths might share cells. What should you
        track instead? How do you prevent infinite loops while still allowing
        multiple paths through the same cell?</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Requirement 4: Subset Generation</h3>

    <p>A different backtracking problem: given {A, B, C}, generate all subsets:</p>

    <pre><code><span class="output">{ }, {A}, {B}, {C}, {A,B}, {A,C}, {B,C}, {A,B,C}</span></code></pre>

    <p>The decision tree: for each element, you either INCLUDE it or EXCLUDE it.
    Two choices per element, N elements → 2^N subsets.</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <p>Which is more efficient: generating all permutations of ABC (6 total)
        or all subsets of ABC (8 total)? What about for 10 elements? Which grows
        faster — n! or 2^n?</p>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Solve a maze using recursive backtracking with visited marking</li>
            <li>Explain why backtracking requires UN-marking cells</li>
            <li>Find all paths through a maze (not just the first one)</li>
            <li>Generate all subsets using include/exclude recursion</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/recursion-basics.html">Recursion Basics</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/trees-and-traversal.html">Trees &amp; Traversal</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-08/2d-array-basics.html">2D Array Basics</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
